# Tempest Auxiliary PCB 037585-01 -- netlist, read by eye
#
# Source: DP-190 sheet 3 Side B, 4th printing ("037585-01 B +", doc ad5d39e0e6ea),
# rendered at 300-600 dpi from the 400 dpi scan. The 4th printing is in colour, so its
# signature-analysis annotations (blue) cannot be mistaken for wire. Every section was
# compared with the 2nd printing (ba4e9f648414) and the dense parts with the 1st
# (a2e9aa09a858, "037585-01 A"); where they differ the comment says so. Every resistor,
# capacitor and diode value was checked against the typeset parts list, TM-190 Figure 22
# (doc 56a2f53adedf pp. 45-46): all 130 agree, R58 after a correction noted below.
# tools/build_board_nets.py reads this file.
#
#   net NAME: PIN PIN ...   a net whose name is printed on the sheet
#   wire PIN PIN ...        an unnamed connection -- same net, no name on the sheet
#   sig PIN CODE [TEST]     a signature printed beside the pin (TEST defaults to 1)
#   nc PIN ...              a pin the sheet draws with nothing attached, or not at all
#   label NAME: PIN ...     an unnamed net we have given a name to (not on the sheet)
#   parallel PIN PIN ...    outputs the sheet ties together on purpose (K3)
#   value REF VALUE         a passive's value as printed
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. ICs are U + the grid position exactly as the sheet prints
# it (UB4, UK/L2); the build files a spanning device under its first cell (UK2), as the
# board map does. Passives as printed (R54, C12, CR1).
#
# The sheet's legend names its connectors: square = edge connector J18 (2 x 15, 1-15
# and A-S, every contact used), hexagon = the interconnect J17 (the cable to the game
# PCB, 24 pins), circle = the signature-analysis header J16. Test points (the legend's
# flag) and the three jumpers are not designated on the sheet: TP1-TP10 and JP1-JP3 are
# ours, and SW1 is ours for the DIP switch the parts list calls D/E2 (it is not an IC,
# so it stays out of the board map). Where a comment says "Uncertain", it records what
# was checked and why the reading was taken.
#
# Power pins of logic ICs are not drawn on this sheet except where noted; the build
# adds them from the part's standard pinout. "P,R1" / "P,R54" printed at a pin means
# pulled up through the 1K R1 or R54 drawn in the Power Inputs box: such pins are
# written onto R1.1 / R54.1.
#
# The sheet was read in four parts, one per titled box: the address decoder with the
# J17 buses and the power inputs; the Math Box; the high-score memory; and the player
# inputs and audio output.

# ===========================================================================
# Auxiliary Board Address Decoder, J17 buses, Power Inputs
# ===========================================================================
#
# Source: DP-190 sheet 3 Side B, 4th printing (ad5d39e0e6ea), read from the 300 dpi
# tiles and 600-900 dpi crops. Every connection
# below was compared against the 2nd printing (ba4e9f648414) and the decoder and J17
# pin numbers against the 1st printing (a2e9aa09a858) as well; all three agree.
# Coordinates in comments are 300 dpi sheet pixels.
#
# Extent. The decoder box is not a rectangle. Its right edge runs down x~2575 only as
# far as y~795, then steps right along y~795 to x~3765 and runs down x~3765: it wraps
# under the A2 (LS109) start box, which belongs to the Math Box frame. So J4 (LS02),
# C5 pins 3-6 (LS10), F4 pins 5/6 (LS14) and C4 pins 11-13 (LS32), drawn at x
# 2790-3000, y 900-1400, are inside the decoder box and are read here.
#
# No IC power pins are drawn in this box. No signatures are printed in it.

# part UB4 74LS139
# part UB5 74LS139
# part UC4 74LS32
# part UC5 74LS10
# part UF4 74LS14
# part UJ4 74LS02

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Game-PCB interconnect J17 (hexagons) -- control lines
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TP1 is the test-point flag labelled "EPHI2" (printed E-phi-2) at (1190,447).
# EPHI2 also runs right along y~520, across the A2 box, and drops down x~2790 to
# J4.6 and C5.4. At (2790,752) a T (no dot, same in all three printings) sends it
# right along y~752 to x~3581 and down: it leaves the decoder box heading down at
# (3581, ~800) -- presumably to the POKEYs' phi-2 pins, which are labelled EPHI2.
# Uncertain: the branch at (2790,752) is a T with no dot. The y~752 line starts at the
# x~2790 vertical and does not continue left of it, in all three printings, so it is
# read as joined. Nothing in this box hangs on it; it matters for whoever owns the
# far end of the x~3581 line.
net EPHI2: J17.7 TP1.1 UC5.11 UJ4.6 UC5.4
# TP2 is the test-point flag labelled "ER/WB" (printed ER/W-bar-B) at (1180,850).
# ER/WB leaves the box heading right along y~1452 from x~1887 (to the POKEYs' R/W).
net ER/WB: J17.8 TP2.1 UF4.11 UC4.4
net /I/O: J17.9 UB4.1 UC4.1
# R/WB is printed with the bar over the R only (R-bar/WB), the inverse of ER/WB.
# Named here without the partial bar, following the ER/WB precedent; it runs only
# inside this box.
# Uncertain: spelling only (the bar covers the R only).
net R/WB: UF4.10 UC5.10 UC4.9
# E3MHZ arrives by label: printed at C5.9 and again at C5.5. The C5.5 line runs up
# x~2676 to y~714 and left to a free end at (1763,714) just under the C5.9 label
# (all three printings draw it that way); it connects to nothing else.
net E3MHZ: UC5.9 UC5.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# External Address Bus "From Sheet 2 Side A" -- J17
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pins as printed beside the hexagons (EAB7 on 5 is not a misreading: all three
# printings agree). The thick bars are bus arrows, not wires: each decoder input is
# named at its own pin. EAB3-EAB0 go to the High Score Memory (A3 LS174) and the
# Math Box (A1) by name. Two short labelled taps (EAB7 at x~1524, EAB6 at x~1576,
# hanging down to y~1222) carry no pins.
net EAB7: J17.5 UB4.3 UB4.13
net EAB6: J17.16 UB4.2 UB4.14
net EAB5: J17.15 UB5.3 UB5.13
net EAB4: J17.14 UB5.2 UB5.14
net EAB3: J17.13
net EAB2: J17.12
net EAB1: J17.11
net EAB0: J17.10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# External Data Bus "From Sheet 2 Side A" -- J17
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hexagons at x~640, y 3880-4250. The double-headed hatched arrow is a bus symbol.
net EDB7: J17.24
net EDB6: J17.23
net EDB5: J17.22
net EDB4: J17.21
net EDB3: J17.20
net EDB2: J17.19
net EDB1: J17.18
net EDB0: J17.17

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decoder -- first level: B4 (upper half) splits the $60xx I/O page on EAB7/EAB6
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The decode matches MAME's Tempest map exactly: EAB7,6 = 00 EAROM write, 01 EAROM
# control / read-back group, 10 Math Box start, 11 POKEYs and output latch.
nc UB4.4
# Y1 (EAB7,6 = 01) gates the read strobes in B5's lower half, with R/WB
wire UB4.5 UC4.10
# Y2 (EAB7,6 = 10) ORed with ER/WB: low on a write to the Math Box start address
wire UB4.6 UC4.5
# Y3 (EAB7,6 = 11) enables B5's upper half
wire UB4.7 UB5.1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decoder -- POKEYs and output latch: B5 upper half on EAB5/EAB4
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /POKEY1 leaves heading down x~2572 from y~1100; /POKEY2 heads down x~2766 from y~1071.
net /POKEY1: UB5.4
net /POKEY2: UB5.5
wire UB5.6 UC4.12
nc UB5.7
# /OL = Y2 ORed with /WRITE. Leaves heading down x~2980 from y~1385.
net /WRITE: UC5.8 UC4.13 UC4.2
net /OL: UC4.11

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decoder -- read strobes: B5 lower half, enabled by C4.8 = B4.Y1 OR R/WB
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
wire UC4.8 UB5.15
# /YHI, /YLO and /EARD leave heading right at y~1531, ~1570 and ~1610 (to the Math
# Box slices' OE and to F4.9 in the High Score Memory); /STREN turns down x~2407
# from y~1650 (to F4.13 in the audio section).
net /YHI: UB5.9
net /YLO: UB5.10
net /EARD: UB5.11
net /STREN: UB5.12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decoder -- EAROM write strobes: B4 lower half, enabled by C4.3 = /I/O OR /WRITE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /WRITE = NAND(EPHI2, E3MHZ, R/WB). Its line runs down x~2147 to a dot at
# (2147,1365), left along y~1365 and down x~1435 to C4.2, and right to C4.13.
wire UC4.3 UB4.15
nc UB4.9 UB4.10
# /EARCON turns down at x~1690 from y~2346 (to J3 CK in the High Score Memory);
# /EARWR leaves heading right along y~2190 (to A3 CK and F3 CK).
net /EARCON: UB4.11
net /EARWR: UB4.12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decoder -- Math Box start and SA clock 3
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# C4.6 is low for a write to EAB7,6 = 10. J4 NORs it with EPHI2 to make M START;
# F4 inverts it for C5, which NANDs it with EPHI2 and E3MHZ into SACLK3.
wire UC4.6 UJ4.5 UF4.5
# M START also runs up x~2977 out of the decoder box to A2 pin 1 (C, clear) of the
# Math Box's LS109 -- UA2.1 is drawn in the Math Box, listed here so the join is explicit.
net M START: UJ4.4 J16.3 UA2.1
wire UF4.6 UC5.3
net SACLK3: UC5.6 J16.8

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gates of F4, J4 and C5 the sheet does not draw anywhere
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Whole sheet scanned: F4's other inverters are 1->2 (Math Box), 9->8 (High Score
# Memory) and 13->12 (audio, /STREN); J4's other drawn gate is 2,3->1 (Math Box).
# These remain, drawn nowhere:
# Uncertain: scanned at 100-300 dpi, not pin by pin; if another box draws one of these
# gates drawn, its reading wins over these nc lines.
nc UF4.3 UF4.4
nc UJ4.8 UJ4.9 UJ4.10 UJ4.11 UJ4.12 UJ4.13
nc UC5.1 UC5.2 UC5.13 UC5.12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Power Inputs -- edge connector J18 (squares)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each supply is drawn arriving on a block of J18 squares: "2|14 over B|R" is +5V,
# "1|15 over A|S" is +5V RET, "4 over N" is -22V, "3 over P" is +22V.
net +5V: J18.2 J18.14 J18.B J18.R C56.1 R2.1 R1.2 R54.2 TP3.1
# Uncertain: J17.2 is not wired on its own; its hexagon is drawn stacked on J17.1's,
# touching, with the ground wire reaching J17.1 only. Read as both pins grounded (the
# same stacking the J18 blocks use). Same in the 2nd printing.
net GND: J18.1 J18.15 J18.A J18.S C56.2 CR1.1 TP4.1 J16.1 J17.1 J17.2
# TP3 is the flag labelled "+5V". TP4 is the flag labelled "(4)GND" -- one flag
# drawn, the "(4)" presumably the number of ground test points on the board.
# The GND line also carries a drawn ground symbol, the SA header's J16.1 ("GND"),
# and interconnect J17.1 with J17.2 drawn as a stacked hexagon touching it.
# CR1 is the power-on LED: anode (pin 2) from R2, cathode (pin 1) to ground
wire R2.2 CR1.2
value C56 100uF 25V
value R2 150
value CR1 MV5053
# "R1,54 1K PULLUP": one resistor symbol for both, the "P," end up, +5V end down.
# Their pulled-up nodes are wherever "P,R1" / "P,R54" are printed (Math Box,
# audio, player inputs) and go on R1.1 / R54.1 there.
value R1 1K
value R54 1K
# -22V: C37's + plate is drawn on the ground side (negative rail)
net -22V: J18.4 J18.N C37.2 TP5.1
net GND: C37.1
# +22V
net +22V: J18.3 J18.P C38.1 TP6.1
net GND: C38.2
# TP5 is the flag labelled "-22V", TP6 the flag labelled "+22V". Both rails leave
# the box as labels ("-22V", "+22V") heading right.
value C37 470uF 25V
value C38 470uF 25V
# C56, C37 and C38 are all printed 25V.

# Bypass: "C1-8, 12, 14, 15, 17, 18, 22-26, 30, 33-36, 41, 44, 49, 54  0.1 BYPASS",
# +5V to GND (27 capacitors). The sheet draws one and lists the rest; where each
# sits is not drawn. Same list in the 2nd printing.
net +5V: C1.1 C2.1 C3.1 C4.1 C5.1 C6.1 C7.1 C8.1 C12.1 C14.1 C15.1 C17.1 C18.1 C22.1 C23.1 C24.1 C25.1 C26.1 C30.1 C33.1 C34.1 C35.1 C36.1 C41.1 C44.1 C49.1 C54.1
net GND: C1.2 C2.2 C3.2 C4.2 C5.2 C6.2 C7.2 C8.2 C12.2 C14.2 C15.2 C17.2 C18.2 C22.2 C23.2 C24.2 C25.2 C26.2 C30.2 C33.2 C34.2 C35.2 C36.2 C41.2 C44.2 C49.2 C54.2
value C1 .1uF
value C2 .1uF
value C3 .1uF
value C4 .1uF
value C5 .1uF
value C6 .1uF
value C7 .1uF
value C8 .1uF
value C12 .1uF
value C14 .1uF
value C15 .1uF
value C17 .1uF
value C18 .1uF
value C22 .1uF
value C23 .1uF
value C24 .1uF
value C25 .1uF
value C26 .1uF
value C30 .1uF
value C33 .1uF
value C34 .1uF
value C35 .1uF
value C36 .1uF
value C41 .1uF
value C44 .1uF
value C49 .1uF
value C54 .1uF

# ===========================================================================
# Math Box
# ===========================================================================
#
# Source: DP-190 sheet 3 Side B, 4th printing (ad5d39e0e6ea), rendered at 300 and 600
# dpi. Every gate, the slice pin columns and the
# sequencer were compared against the 2nd printing (ba4e9f648414); the BEGIN routing
# and the slice block against the 1st (a2e9aa09a858) too. Where they differ it says so.
# Coordinates in comments are 300 dpi sheet pixels.
#
# Extent. The Math Box frame (7 px rules, twice a wire's weight) runs along y~140 from
# x~2688 to x~10015, down the right edge x~10015 to y~3412, back along y~3412 to
# x~3868, up x~3868 to y~684, left along y~684 to x~2688 and up to the top: the
# A2 / D4 start logic sits in the notch at top left. J4.4, F4.6, C5 and C4, drawn at
# x 2790-3000 below y~684, are inside the address decoder's frame (the decoder box), not here.
# The line along y~3412 is this frame's bottom edge, not a
# wire (it is 6-7 px like the other three edges, and turns up x~10015 into the top
# edge at y~140); /EARWR (x~3941) and /EARD (x~3907) cross it without a dot.
#
# Drawing conventions on this sheet that matter here:
#  * A pin number is written outside the body, above its wire: just left of the edge
#    for an input on the left, just right of the edge for an output on the right.
#  * The four slices are drawn as a row of abutting boxes E2 | J2 | F/H2 | K/L2. An
#    input line crosses all four boxes; its pin number is written once, at E2's left
#    edge, and applies to every box the line enters (the line ends at K/L2's left edge,
#    where its name is repeated inside K/L2: CP, A19 ... A0, GND). Where a line feeds
#    only some boxes the number is repeated at those boxes (pin 14: A8 to E2 and J2,
#    A9 to F/H2 and K/L2). Each box's Y/D pins and OE are drawn on its right edge.
#  * The six PROMs are drawn the same way: address pin numbers written once, left of
#    L1, the address lines running through L1 K1 J1 H1 F1 E1; "A7 ... A0" printed
#    inside E1's box are the PROM's address-pin names, not the microcode nets A0-A7.
#  * Labels are usually in-line (a wire runs into a name and out the other side).
#
# Power pins are drawn only for the slices' GND (pin 30) and the PROM enables.
#
# Legend: circle = SA connector J16, hexagon = interconnect J17, pentagon flag
# = test point; TP7 is ours.

# part UA2 74109 (printed "109" with no family letter; the board map has 74109)
# part UD4 74LS00
# part UJ4 74LS02 (gate 2,3->1 only; 5,6->4 is in the decoder box)
# part UF4 74LS14 (inverter 1->2 only; 5->6 the decoder box, 9->8 the High Score Memory box, 13->12 the Player Inputs box)
# part UD5 74LS74
# part UE4 74S08 (gates 4,5->6; 9,10->8; 12,13->11)
# part UE5 74S86 (gates 9,10->8; 12,13->11)
# part UF5 74S03 (gates 12,13->11; 4,5->6; 9,10->8 is in the Player Inputs box)
# part UB1 74LS374 (jump-address latch)
# part UA1 136002-126 (82S123 entry PROM)
# part UD1 74LS161 (program counter, low)
# part UC1 74LS161 (program counter, high)
# part UL1 136002-132 (82S129 microcode PROM)
# part UK1 136002-131 (82S129 microcode PROM)
# part UJ1 136002-130 (82S129 microcode PROM)
# part UH1 136002-129 (82S129 microcode PROM)
# part UF1 136002-128 (82S129 microcode PROM)
# part UE1 136002-127 (82S129 microcode PROM)
# part UE2 137004-001 (Am2901 bit slice; most significant, bits 15-12)
# part UJ2 137004-001 (Am2901 bit slice; bits 11-8)
# part UF/H2 137004-001 (Am2901 bit slice; bits 7-4)
# part UK/L2 137004-001 (Am2901 bit slice; least significant, bits 3-0)
value R42 470

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Start: A2 makes BEGIN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A2 is held clear while M START is low; then on an E6MHZ edge with E3MHZ high it sets
# (BEGIN), and its own /BEGIN on /K clears it again on the next edge.
# M START: A2.1's line runs down x~3245, left along y~650 and down x~2978, out of the
# notch through the frame at (2978,684) into the decoder box, where it lands on J4.4 and the
# SA circle (3) printed "M START" (same in the 2nd and 1st printings). the decoder box lists
# UA2.1 on M START as well.
net M START: UA2.1
net E3MHZ: UA2.2 UJ4.2
# E6MHZ: the hexagon (3) and the pentagon flag printed "E6MHZ" join at a dot (3035,465).
# TP7: test-point flag printed "E6MHZ", at (2840-2990,580).
net E6MHZ: J17.3 UA2.4 TP7.1
# R1 is a 1K pull-up drawn in the Power Inputs box; the sheet prints "P,R1" here
wire UA2.5 R1.1
net BEGIN: UA2.6 UD4.2
net /BEGIN: UA2.7 UA2.3
sig UA2.6 0000
# Uncertain: B1.1 (the latch's /OC) is printed "BEGIN" at the pin, but the wire drawn below
# that label runs down x~4703, left along y~1542, up x~3946 and left along y~566 into
# the in-line "/BEGIN" label on A2's /Q line -- so as drawn A2.7 reaches a pin labelled
# BEGIN. The same in all three printings. Taken as BEGIN: that is the name printed at
# the pin, A2.6's own line runs only to D4.2 and up into a "BEGIN" label that nothing
# else answers, and /BEGIN on both B1.1 and A1.15 would have the latch and the entry
# PROM driving the counter bus together. (Battlezone's latch C1.1 is BEGIN.) If the
# wire is right and the label wrong, move UB1.1 to /BEGIN.
net BEGIN: UB1.1
# The other /BEGIN loads are on a separate drawn line, labelled "/BEGIN" at each end:
# up x~3980 to y~561 and right to D5.13; a T at y~1581 right to A1.15; down to y~2928
# and right along it (across the fold) to E4.12.
net /BEGIN: UD5.13 UA1.15 UE4.12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stop and clock
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D5's second half is the STOP flip-flop: A12 clocked in on /CLK, cleared by /BEGIN.
# CLK runs while STOP is low: J4 NORs STOP with E3MHZ; F4 makes /CLK.
net +5V: R42.2
wire UD5.10 UD5.4 UD5.1 R54.1
# R54 is the other 1K pull-up in the Power Inputs box; the sheet prints "P,R54" at all three
net A12: UD5.12
net STOP: UD5.9 UJ4.3 J16.4
nc UD5.8
net CLK: UJ4.1 UF4.1 UD4.1 UD5.3 UD1.2 UC1.2 UE2.15 UJ2.15 UF/H2.15 UK/L2.15 J16.6
# The SA circle (6) on CLK is printed "SACLK1"
net SACLK1: J16.6
net /CLK: UF4.2 UD5.11 UE4.4
# CC34 is printed beside "/CLK" at F4.2 in both the 4th and 2nd printings
sig UF4.2 CC34
# SA clock 2: CLK gated by BEGIN
net SACLK2: UD4.3 J16.7

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sequencer: entry PROM A1, jump latch B1, program counter D1 (low) / C1 (high)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A1 (addressed by EAB0-4, enabled by /BEGIN) and B1 (enabled by BEGIN) share one bus
# into the counters' parallel inputs. B1 latches microcode A0-A7 on E4.6 = /CLK AND LDAB.
net EAB0: UA1.10
net EAB1: UA1.11
net EAB2: UA1.12
net EAB3: UA1.13
net EAB4: UA1.14
wire UE4.6 UB1.11
net LDAB: UE4.5
wire UB1.19 UA1.1 UD1.3
wire UB1.2 UA1.2 UD1.4
wire UB1.16 UA1.3 UD1.5
wire UB1.5 UA1.4 UD1.6
wire UB1.15 UA1.5 UC1.3
wire UB1.6 UA1.6 UC1.4
wire UB1.12 UA1.7 UC1.5
wire UB1.9 UA1.9 UC1.6
# Counters: /PCEN loads both (LD) and gates the count (ET); "P,R1" on D1's EP and both clears
net /PCEN: UE4.11 UD1.9 UD1.10 UC1.9 UC1.10
wire UD1.7 UD1.1 UC1.1 R1.1
wire UD1.15 UC1.7
nc UC1.15
# Microcode address bus: every PROM sees the same eight lines (numbers printed once,
# left of L1: 15 1 2 3 4 7 6 5 from MA7 down to MA0)
label MA0: UD1.14 UL1.5 UK1.5 UJ1.5 UH1.5 UF1.5 UE1.5
label MA1: UD1.13 UL1.6 UK1.6 UJ1.6 UH1.6 UF1.6 UE1.6
label MA2: UD1.12 UL1.7 UK1.7 UJ1.7 UH1.7 UF1.7 UE1.7
label MA3: UD1.11 UL1.4 UK1.4 UJ1.4 UH1.4 UF1.4 UE1.4
label MA4: UC1.14 UL1.3 UK1.3 UJ1.3 UH1.3 UF1.3 UE1.3
label MA5: UC1.13 UL1.2 UK1.2 UJ1.2 UH1.2 UF1.2 UE1.2
label MA6: UC1.12 UL1.1 UK1.1 UJ1.1 UH1.1 UF1.1 UE1.1
label MA7: UC1.11 UL1.15 UK1.15 UJ1.15 UH1.15 UF1.15 UE1.15
# The SA circle (9) printed "SAEN1" taps MA7 with a dot at (6012,1683)
net SAEN1: J16.9 UC1.11
# The six PROMs are always enabled
net GND: UL1.13 UL1.14 UK1.13 UK1.14 UJ1.13 UJ1.14 UH1.13 UH1.14 UF1.13 UF1.14 UE1.13 UE1.14

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Microcode fields -- the PROM outputs, by the sheet's own names
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each PROM's outputs 12, 11, 10, 9 run right (across the PROMs to its right) to a
# name at x~7130-7290, then through the crossbar to the slices (names repeated at
# x~7800 left of E2), B1 and the control gates.
net A0: UL1.9 UB1.8 UE2.1 UJ2.1 UF/H2.1 UK/L2.1
net A1: UL1.10 UB1.13 UE2.2 UJ2.2 UF/H2.2 UK/L2.2
net A2: UL1.11 UB1.7 UE2.3 UJ2.3 UF/H2.3 UK/L2.3
net A3: UL1.12 UB1.14 UE2.4 UJ2.4 UF/H2.4 UK/L2.4
net A4: UK1.9 UB1.4 UE2.20 UJ2.20 UF/H2.20 UK/L2.20
net A5: UK1.10 UB1.17 UE2.19 UJ2.19 UF/H2.19 UK/L2.19
net A6: UK1.11 UB1.3 UE2.18 UJ2.18 UF/H2.18 UK/L2.18
net A7: UK1.12 UB1.18 UE2.17 UJ2.17 UF/H2.17 UK/L2.17
net A8: UJ1.9 UE2.14 UJ2.14
net A9: UJ1.10 UF/H2.14 UK/L2.14
# A10 goes up x~7519 and back along y~424 to E5.9; A10* (E5.8) feeds pin 13 of all
# four slices. The slice line is printed "A10*" left of E2 but "A10" at its K/L2 end
# (all printings) -- one continuous line, so it is A10* throughout.
net A10: UJ1.11 UE5.9
net A10*: UE5.8 UE2.13 UJ2.13 UF/H2.13 UK/L2.13
net A11: UJ1.12 UE2.12 UJ2.12 UF/H2.12 UK/L2.12
net A12: UH1.9
net A13: UH1.10 UE2.27 UJ2.27 UF/H2.27 UK/L2.27
net A14: UH1.11 UE2.28 UJ2.28 UF/H2.28 UK/L2.28
net A15: UH1.12 UE2.26 UJ2.26 UF/H2.26 UK/L2.26
# LDAB (Battlezone's A16) runs up x~7590, left along y~153 and down x~3916 to E4.5
net LDAB: UF1.9
net A17: UF1.10 UE2.6 UJ2.6 UF/H2.6 UK/L2.6
net A18: UF1.11 UE2.7 UJ2.7 UF/H2.7 UK/L2.7 UD4.4 UD4.5 UF5.12 UF5.13
net A19: UF1.12 UE2.5 UJ2.5 UF/H2.5 UK/L2.5
# E1's four outputs are printed C, M, J, S (Battlezone's A23, A22, A21, A20)
net S: UE1.9 UD4.9
net J: UE1.10 UD4.12 J16.5
net M: UE1.11 UE4.9
net C: UE1.12 UK/L2.29

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The four 137004-001 slices -- data bus and output enables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# E2 (MS) and J2 drive the high byte under /YHI, F/H2 and K/L2 the low byte under
# /YLO: E2 and F/H2 onto EDB7-4, J2 and K/L2 onto EDB3-0. Each slice's D inputs are
# tied to its own Y outputs (22-39, 23-38, 24-37, 25-36), joined by dots at x~9073
# (E2 + F/H2) and x~9430 (J2 + K/L2). The names "D7 ... D0" printed inside E2's and
# J2's boxes beside these pins are the symbol's, not net names.
net EDB7: UE2.22 UE2.39 UF/H2.22 UF/H2.39
net EDB6: UE2.23 UE2.38 UF/H2.23 UF/H2.38
net EDB5: UE2.24 UE2.37 UF/H2.24 UF/H2.37
net EDB4: UE2.25 UE2.36 UF/H2.25 UF/H2.36
net EDB3: UJ2.22 UJ2.39 UK/L2.22 UK/L2.39
net EDB2: UJ2.23 UJ2.38 UK/L2.23 UK/L2.38
net EDB1: UJ2.24 UJ2.37 UK/L2.24 UK/L2.37
net EDB0: UJ2.25 UJ2.36 UK/L2.25 UK/L2.36
# /YHI and /YLO: printed at the arrowheads right of the slices; each runs down the
# right side (x~9727, x~9692) and back left along y~3050 / y~3010 out through the
# frame at x~3868 into the decoder box (B5.9 / B5.10).
net /YHI: UE2.40 UJ2.40
net /YLO: UF/H2.40 UK/L2.40
net GND: UE2.30 UJ2.30 UF/H2.30 UK/L2.30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Carry, RAM-shift and Q-shift chains, least significant slice to most
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# K/L2 -> F/H2 -> J2 -> E2. Drawn as three nested loops under each pair of boxes.
wire UK/L2.33 UF/H2.29
wire UF/H2.33 UJ2.29
wire UJ2.33 UE2.29
wire UK/L2.8 UF/H2.9
wire UF/H2.8 UJ2.9
wire UJ2.8 UE2.9
wire UK/L2.16 UF/H2.21
wire UF/H2.16 UJ2.21
wire UJ2.16 UE2.21
# Ends of the chains, all labelled under the pins:
# R0: E2's Q3 into K/L2's RAM0 (double-length shift), along y~2273
net R0: UE2.16 UK/L2.9
# Q0: K/L2's Q0, driven open-collector from F5.11 (/A18), latched by D5 for multiply
net Q0: UK/L2.21 UF5.11 UD5.2
# R15: E2's RAM3, along y~2237 and down x~9085 to F5.6 / R42 (pull-up to +5V)
net R15: UE2.8 UF5.6 R42.1
# S1, S0: E2's F3 and OVR, to the sign XOR
net S1: UE2.31 UE5.12
net S0: UE2.34 UE5.13

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign, shift and multiply logic (bottom right)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# E5.11 = F3 XOR OVR (true sign). D4.8 = NAND(S, sign); D4.11 = NAND(J, D4.8);
# /PCEN = E4.11 = D4.11 AND /BEGIN, so the counter loads at BEGIN and on a taken jump.
# F5.6 drives RAM3 of the MS slice (shift-in) from D4.6 (/A18) and D4.8.
wire UE5.11 UD4.10
wire UD4.8 UD4.13 UF5.4
wire UD4.11 UE4.13
wire UD4.6 UF5.5
# Multiply: D5's first half latches Q0 on CLK; E4.8 = /Q AND M; E5.8 = A10 XOR that
wire UD5.6 UE4.10
wire UE4.8 UE5.10
nc UD5.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pins the sheet draws with nothing attached
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# E2's Cn+4 (the top of the carry chain)
nc UE2.33
# F=0, /G, /P: drawn as bare stubs at E2's left edge (11, 32, 35) and not drawn for
# the other three; F3 and OVR (31, 34) bare on J2, F/H2 and K/L2
nc UE2.11 UE2.32 UE2.35
nc UJ2.11 UJ2.31 UJ2.32 UJ2.34 UJ2.35
nc UF/H2.11 UF/H2.31 UF/H2.32 UF/H2.34 UF/H2.35
nc UK/L2.11 UK/L2.31 UK/L2.32 UK/L2.34 UK/L2.35
# Not drawn in this box, nor in any other (listed with the undrawn pins at the end):
# A2 pins 9-15 (the LS109's second flip-flop), E4 gate 1,2->3, E5 gates 1,2->3 and
# 4,5->6, F5 gate 1,2->3.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wires that only pass through the Math Box (no pin here). All are named in the decoder box.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# entering at the left frame x~3868 (from the decoder box) and leaving down through y~3412:
#   y~1608, down x~3907, out at (3907,3412)   the decoder box: /EARD; the High Score Memory box: to F4.9
#   y~2188, down x~3941, out at (3941,3412)   the decoder box: /EARWR; the High Score Memory box: A3, F3 CK
#   y~3112, down x~8087, out at (8087,3412)   the Player Inputs box: /STREN to F4.13
#   y~3151, down x~8052, out at (8052,3412)
#   y~3202, down x~7109, out at (7109,3412)
#   y~3237, down x~7066, out at (7066,3412)
#   y~3274, down x~6959, out at (6959,3412)
#   y~3306, down x~5746, out at (5746,3412)
# entering the notch at (2688,521), down x~2790, out at (2790,684): the decoder box's EPHI2.
# The black EAB bus bar along y~3340-3385 also crosses (to the POKEYs), and the grey
# EDB bus leaves down the right side x~9915-9990.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signatures (Test #1 unless named)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROM outputs, printed between E1 and the names
sig UL1.9 6CF6
sig UL1.10 F74F
sig UL1.11 1AA2
sig UL1.12 58A1
sig UK1.9 34C2
sig UK1.10 PPF6
sig UK1.11 60H6
sig UK1.12 05A6
sig UJ1.9 3FU4
sig UJ1.10 6989
sig UJ1.11 UC59
sig UJ1.12 96U0
sig UH1.9 2987
sig UH1.10 P9C1
sig UH1.11 91HA
sig UH1.12 26A6
sig UF1.9 A6A3
sig UF1.10 3CAP
sig UF1.11 0UF0
sig UF1.12 4PPF
sig UE1.9 F515
sig UE1.10 0000
sig UE1.11 CPU8
sig UE1.12 F765
# B1 outputs
sig UB1.9 6U30
sig UB1.12 5AAH
sig UB1.6 1C6C
sig UB1.15 03A7
sig UB1.5 CU2P
sig UB1.16 9A08
sig UB1.2 8A7H
sig UB1.19 2327
# Control logic. "*" = taken with the 1K pull-up on the probe; the asterisk is
